\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The crossing block sets the reflection rank}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, written
as an orthogonal sum #{V=V_+\oplus V_-}. Either summand may be the
zero space. Let #{i_\pm} and #{p_\pm} be the inclusions and the
orthogonal projections, and let #{Q:V\to V} be self-adjoint and
positive definite. Write #{Q_{\varepsilon\eta}=p_\varepsilon Q i_\eta}.
Let #{C=Q^{-1}} and write #{C_{\varepsilon\eta}} in the same way.
The compressions #{Q_{++}} and #{C_{{-}{-}}} are invertible, and
##{\operatorname{rank}C_{+-}=\operatorname{rank}Q_{+-}.}
The zero map between zero spaces has rank zero, so the identity
includes a zero summand.}

\p{On the even torus of the [reflection halves](fdip-007M), with the
spatial factor a point, take the link reflection and the
[massive scalar law](fdip-007O) of nearest-neighbor weight #{1} and
mass #{m>0}. The reflected bilinear form on the positive-half
linear observables has rank #{2} and is positive semidefinite. In
the coordinate basis of that half, the form is the crossing block
of #{C} with columns ordered by the link reflection. The normalized Gaussian transfer operator remains the
injective positive contraction of the [Hermite diagonalization](fdip-007U).
The gauge transfer operator remains positive, injective and trace
class, as proved for the [spatial transfer data](fdip-0080).}

\p{Let #{H} be a self-adjoint operator on a Hermitian space of finite
dimension #{n\ge 1}, and let #{\beta} be real. This dimension
hypothesis belongs only to the Gibbs state: the rank identity above
does not use it. Write #{\rho=e^{-\beta H}/Z} with #{Z=\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\beta H})},
and write #{\sigma(A)=e^{-\beta H}Ae^{\beta H}}. The Gibbs state
#{\omega(A)=\operatorname{Tr}(\rho A)} satisfies
##{\omega(AB)=\omega(B\sigma(A)).}}

\proof{
\p{Use the orthogonal splitting as the declared block basis. If
#{V_+} is the zero space, then #{Q_{++}} is the identity
endomorphism of that space, hence invertible, and there is no
nonzero vector in it. If #{v} is a nonzero vector in #{V_+},
extend it by zero on #{V_-}. Positive definiteness of #{Q} gives
#{v^TQ_{++}v>0}, so #{Q_{++}} is positive definite and invertible.
The same argument with the roles exchanged shows that #{C_{{-}{-}}}
is invertible, including when #{V_-} is zero. The identity
#{p_+QC i_-=p_+i_-=0} is the block equation
##{Q_{++}C_{+-}+Q_{+-}C_{{-}{-}}=0.}
Hence
##{C_{+-}=-Q_{++}^{-1}Q_{+-}C_{{-}{-}}.}
Composition on the left with the invertible map #{Q_{++}^{-1}} and
on the right with the invertible map #{C_{{-}{-}}} preserves rank,
including when #{C_{+-}} or #{Q_{+-}} is the zero map. The two
compressions therefore have the same rank.}

\p{For the cycle, the positive half is #{\{1,\ldots,M\}} and its
image under #{\theta_\ell(t)=1-t} is the complementary half, with
#{M\ge 2} and length #{2M}. The quadratic form of the massive
scalar law has off-diagonal entry #{-1} on each nearest-neighbor
edge and no other off-diagonal entry. The only edges with one end
in each half are #{\{0,1\}} and #{\{M,M+1\}}. Their rows in the
positive half are the distinct sites #{1} and #{M}, and their
columns in the negative half are the distinct sites #{0} and
#{M+1}. The crossing block therefore has rank #{2}. The covariance
crossing block has the same rank. For real coordinate functions
#{x_a} on the positive half,
#{\Theta x_a=x_{\theta(a)}}, so the reflection Gram matrix is that
crossing block of #{C} with columns ordered by #{\theta}. Its rank is
#{2}. Every positive-half polynomial lies in the reflection-positive
domain of the massive scalar law, so the Gram matrix is positive
semidefinite.}

\p{Choose an orthonormal basis of the Hermitian space. Its dimension
#{n} is at least #{1}, and the matrix of #{H} in that basis is
Hermitian. Let #{\mu} bound the absolute values of the entries of
that matrix.
By induction, the entries of #{H^k} are at most #{n^{k-1}\mu^k}.
The exponential series is therefore dominated by a scalar
exponential and converges entrywise. Since #{H} commutes with
#{-H}, the Cauchy product of the series for #{e^{\beta H}} and
#{e^{-\beta H}} is the series for #{e^{0}}, which is the identity.
The coefficients are real and #{H^*=H}, so #{e^{-\beta H}} is
Hermitian. The same product of series gives
#{e^{-\beta H}=e^{-\beta H/2}e^{-\beta H/2}}. For a column
#{v}, the squared length #{v^*e^{-\beta H}v} vanishes only if
#{e^{-\beta H/2}v=0}, hence only if #{v=0}. The exponential is
positive definite, so its trace #{Z} is positive. For finite
matrices #{\operatorname{Tr}(XY)=\operatorname{Tr}(YX)}. Cycling
the first factor gives
##{\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\beta H}Be^{-\beta H}Ae^{\beta H})
=\operatorname{Tr}(Be^{-\beta H}A),}
and cycling #{B} gives #{\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\beta H}AB)}.
Dividing by #{Z} is the identity for #{\omega}.}
}

\p{The rank identity uses no reflection. Reflection positivity of
the cycle is the massive scalar theorem already cited, and the
transfer operators are the two theorems already cited. No infinite
volume, no continuum limit, and no claim that the finite Gibbs
identity determines a modular operator beyond the displayed
conjugation are made here.}
